From: Caboose <caboose@local>
Subject: [PATCH 72/83] Batch inline uniform block writes and follow each executor's batched sets

A batched commit could not carry inline uniform block writes (the guest aborted
on them), so batching had to hide inline uniform blocks, which DXVK requires.
A binding's inline bytes are now kept whole in the guest (each write lands at its
offset, from vkUpdateDescriptorSets and from descriptor update templates alike) and the commit sends them as one write with its
VkWriteDescriptorSetInlineUniformBlock. Whether descriptor sets are batched is
read from the connected executor's grant (hello 0x80000, SocketStream.cpp) at
every use, so a process that outlives its executor follows the next one. Sets bound with
vkCmdBindDescriptorSets2(KHR) (maintenance6, which DXVK uses) are recorded for the commit too. A pool of more than 512 sets asks for its ids as before
(0071 deferred them all): a reply that size is not left unread behind the stream's other
deferred replies. The commit takes the encoder's lock: other threads share the encoder, and
DXVK creates objects on them while it submits, so an unlocked commit interleaved with their
packets and replies.
---
